version: "2.0"
description: >-
  Gnomon — the Paradigm 8.0 kernel (docs/specs/gnomon.md). One daemon per user
  owning sessions, identity, the hash-chained log, approvals, the budget fuse
  and the localhost web endpoint; bins gnomon, gnomond and gnomon-web. PR-0
  SCAFFOLD (research 29 §1/§4): the §18 layout with a src/ prefix, placeholder
  bins that print their version, the import fence, the zero-deps check, the
  G-WIN-05 registry skeleton and the M1.0 probe harness. Node >= 24 for Gnomon
  packages only; private and outside the root build/test scripts until the 8.0
  cut (G-MIG-01) — use `npm run build:gnomon` / `npm run test:gnomon`. Will ship
  later as a signed Node SEA (tsup emits one self-contained file per entry).

components:
  gnomon-kernel:
    description: >-
      The kernel package. ZERO runtime deps (G-SEC-01): package.json has no
      `dependencies` key; @a-company/gnomon-contracts is a devDependency that
      tsup bundles. Import fence (G-KRN-02): every import under src/kernel/**
      is node:*, relative, or @a-company/gnomon-contracts, and dist/ holds no
      bare import. src/kernel/ has the thirteen §18 modules (identity, log,
      runner, supervisor, broker, budget, scheduler, manifest, refs, socket,
      web, sandbox, modhost) as empty directories at PR-0. Placeholder bins
      write to stdio directly: the kernel cannot depend on the Paradigm logger
      (zero deps); #kernel-log brings its own logging at M1.2. PR-5 adds
      src/kernel/lifecycle/ — the $daemon-lifecycle core (spec §2.4): home
      resolution (~/.gnomon; GNOMON_HOME is a test seam only, a detached daemon
      gets --home in argv) created 0700 with the mode read back; the endpoint
      path (<home>/gnomon.sock, $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR fallback on Linux on sun_path
      overflow, never /tmp, refuse if nothing fits; win32 refuses pending the
      P8-D78 parity phase); the O_EXCL daemonPid:v1 pidfile lock lifted from
      warpline lifecycle.ts (live holder refuses, dead holder cleaned by
      rename-and-inode-check and re-raced, empty file = racer mid-write); the
      clean re-exec env (PATH fixed pending root-signed config, HOME/USERPROFILE,
      LANG, LC_*; NODE_OPTIONS and GNOMON_* dropped); the idle-exit monitor
      (holders = open #socket connections until sessions/subscribers exist).
      bins: `gnomond [--foreground] [--home] [--idle-ms]`, `gnomon daemon
      status|stop|--detach` (stop refuses not-implemented pending
      #approval-broker, M1.6). Lifecycle events are emitted to a callback using
      the spec §9.1 kinds daemon.start, daemon.exit{reason}, daemon.lock.refused
      (the kernel log takes them at M1.2); the spec declares no signals.
      SYMBOL CONVENTION (P8-D81, test/symbols-drift.test.ts): every source
      module names the symbol(s) it implements in its header (first 40 lines),
      one `@symbol <id>` per line, using the spec Appendix A id verbatim where
      the spec has one (e.g. `@symbol #kernel-log`, `@symbol $daemon-lifecycle`).
      Every .ts under src/kernel/** must carry a tag; every tag must be
      declared in a packages/gnomon*/.purpose; every declared symbol must be an
      Appendix A node or be listed in test/symbol-allowlist.json with a reason.
    path: src/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, paradigm-8, scaffold]
    components:
      - "#gnomon-probes"
      - "#socket"
      - "#kernel-log"
    tests:
      - test/symbols-drift.test.ts
      - test/fence.test.ts
      - test/sec-01.test.ts
      - test/platform-registry.test.ts
      - test/version.test.ts
      - test/daemon-lifecycle.test.ts
      - test/idle-exit.test.ts
      - test/reexec-env.test.ts
    oracles: [G-KRN-02, G-SEC-01, G-WIN-05, G-DMN-01, G-DMN-02]

  gnomon-probes:
    description: >-
      M1.0 probe harness (spec §19.1, research 29 §2). `node
      packages/gnomon/probes/run.mjs <id> [--out-dir d] [--timeout ms]` imports
      probes/<id>/probe.mjs (zero deps, no build), runs its default export with
      {id, platform, arch, probeDir, signal}, and writes a probeResult:v1 record
      to probes/results/<id>.<platform>.json. Throws and timeouts become verdict
      `fail`. Exit codes 0 pass / 1 fail / 2 partial / 64 usage. Probe ids match
      ^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*$ (no path traversal). p-harness is the self-test and
      its results are gitignored; real results are committed from hardware runs
      only. A `fail` on a ruling means a re-plan memo before dependent builds.
      Unsigned macOS probes: p10m-sbx-deny-mac (S29-03/04 sandbox-exec session
      denies + escape matrix), p11-ws-framing (RFC 6455 over node:http with a
      forked server child, 27 Autobahn-style raw cases, 4 MiB throughput, RSS
      and phys_footprint), p-noise-vectors (Noise IK/XX/XXpsk3 over node:crypto
      against a verbatim cacophony/snow vector subset in vectors.json, G-LNK-11)
      p-log-fsync (kernel-log head-anchor fsync stalls, arms A-G; arm G =
      the research 33 policy: coalesced anchor + forced fullfsync+anchor at
      1/64, judged by the restated G-LOG-04; env P_LOG_FSYNC_ARMS accepts
      A,E,G) and p01-claude-cli (read-only `claude --version`/`--help` flag check;
      live items pending-manual).
    path: probes/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, probes, m1.0]
    tests:
      - test/probe-harness.test.ts

  socket:
    description: >-
      #socket (spec §2.1, §4.9) — the kernel's gnomon-rpc:v1 endpoint. PR-5
      core: unix-socket server bound under a 0177 umask, chmod 0600 and the
      ACL read back (socket type, owner uid, mode) before serving; byte-based
      NDJSON framing (4 MiB max frame, partial-line buffering that is UTF-8
      safe across chunks, an oversize line discarded to its newline and
      answered BAD_REQUEST once, malformed UTF-8/JSON/non-object lines refused
      BAD_REQUEST, connection kept open); per-connection in-order replies;
      local minimal frame/refusal shapes (contracts are rewritten in parallel —
      no contract import) using the §4.9 code map. Verbs: `hello` answers a
      refusal-shaped ENGINE not-implemented (M1.4 brings negotiation and
      mutual auth), `daemon.status` and `daemon.stop` (refused pending
      #approval-broker) are pre-ABI placeholders; anything else UNKNOWN_VERB.
      Emits the §9.1 kinds socket.connect and socket.frame.refused{code} to a
      callback; connect/disconnect drive $daemon-lifecycle idle accounting.
      Windows named pipe: parity phase (P8-D78).
    path: src/kernel/socket/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, socket, paradigm-8, pr-5]
    tests:
      - test/ndjson-framing.test.ts
      - test/socket-server.test.ts
    oracles: [G-DMN-02]

  kernel-log:
    description: >-
      #kernel-log (spec §3.2, M1.2; research 29 §4 PR-4) — the one
      hash-chained, append-only, segmented kernel log. node:sqlite; active
      segment <home>/kernel.db, sealed segments <home>/log/segments/NNNNNN.db
      (0600, dirs 0700); `chain` table per §3.2 plus a write-once `meta`;
      BEFORE UPDATE/DELETE triggers abort (L1). hash = sha256(canonical(envelope
      minus hash, sig)), prev(1) = sha256("gnomon-log-genesis:" + logId), seq
      computed inside the write txn and checked against the in-memory tip.
      Envelope = contracts event:v1, validated strict with default-label check;
      unregistered kinds refused (never guessed), labels tighten-only (L6),
      kernel/userland ownership enforced (L7), daemon.segment reserved to the
      log, session.inject must carry {origin, hash, bytes}. Signer seam
      (signer.ts): Signer.sign(domain, message) prepends the §3.1 domain
      (gnomon:event-sig:v1 etc.), key classes limited to their domains;
      SoftwareEd25519Signer = the in-memory kernel key (keyId
      gkey:v1:<sha256(spki PEM)>); root/approvals keys are hardware-only and
      plug in after probes P04/P02; KeyRing verifies Ed25519 and P-256.
      Unsigned rows only for daemon/socket/schedule; the §3.2 mandatory list
      refuses unsigned (unsigned-mandatory). Segments rotate at 10⁶ rows with a
      signed daemon.segment closing row and an unbroken chain across files;
      crash mid-rotation is completed on open. Anchors: file <home>/head
      (atomic tmp+rename; fsync only at genesis/rotation/close) and a
      KeystoreAnchorStore SEAM that reports `unavailable` until the helper
      exists (AnchorStore.write may be async; keystore writes are serialised).
      ANCHOR POLICY (anchor-policy.ts; research 33 S33-05..07, P8-D82):
      `append` is async and resolves {seq, hash, anchored, event}. Bulk rows
      coalesce the file anchor (renamed when 63 rows are un-anchored or 50 ms
      after the first, unref'd timer + inline check, and on checkpoint) and
      queue the keystore anchor every 1023 rows / 1 s off the append path.
      CRITICAL_ANCHOR_KINDS (approval.decide/suspect/presence.denied/
      rule.learned/voice.arm/voice.disarm, key.*, grant.*, config.*,
      manifest.*, billing.*, verdict.*, link pair/renew/revoke,
      session.inject{origin:'peer'}, socket.auth.theft) commit with
      `PRAGMA fullfsync=ON` for that one transaction, rename the file anchor
      and AWAIT the keystore write before resolving {anchored: true}; no
      effect may be released before that (a failed keystore write rejects
      ENGINE{anchor-failed}). Close writes a durable anchor carrying the
      clean-close marker `closed: true`; open rewrites it durably without.
      `forcedAnchorRate` / `anchorStatus` = status.log telemetry (forced
      anchors per row over the last 10⁴ rows; > 1/64 = outside the measured
      envelope). Open refuses INTEGRITY_BROKEN on missing-db (with anchor,
      sealed segment or keys present), truncated (either anchor ahead of the
      db), anchor-mismatch, anchor-missing, and anchor-lag (file anchor > 63
      rows behind, or any lag behind a clean-close anchor); a smaller lag is
      accepted only as a chain prefix and repaired; keystore lag > 1023
      appends a kernel-signed daemon.anchor.lag{rows, which}
      (contracts kind added). Fsync
      policy: WAL + synchronous=FULL (default 'process-crash'); 'power-loss'
      adds fullfsync (~10 ms/commit on Apple SSD, above the 5 ms falsifier).
      verify(): full chain (segment links, genesis, anchors) or one segment;
      detects tamper, re-hash forgery, truncation, reorder, stripped or bad
      signatures, unknown keys, relaxed labels. read(): §9.3 projection per
      reader class (human, read-token, community-client, session, mod, peer;
      agent/voice refuse UNSUPPORTED pending M1.7/M4.x). Not yet: async
      hardware signers, helper anchor, blobs/TTL pruning, projections tables,
      wiring $daemon-lifecycle events into the log.
    path: src/kernel/log/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, log, paradigm-8, pr-4, m1.2]
    aspects: ["~ample-internal-logs"]
    tests:
      - test/kernel-log.test.ts
      - test/kernel-log-posix.test.ts
    oracles: [G-LOG-01, G-LOG-02, G-LOG-03, G-LOG-04, G-LOG-06, G-LOG-07, G-LOG-08, G-LOG-09, G-LOG-10]

flows:
  daemon-lifecycle:
    description: >-
      $daemon-lifecycle (spec §2.4; PR-5 core of M1.9). (1) client activation —
      `gnomon daemon --detach` re-execs gnomond with the clean env and home in
      argv, then waits for the socket; (2) one per user — home 0700 read back,
      endpoint path chosen (never /tmp), O_EXCL daemonPid:v1 pidfile acquired
      (live holder refuses with daemon.lock.refused, dead holder reclaimed);
      (3) #socket listens at 0600 and daemon.start is emitted; (4) idle exit —
      no open connections for idleMs (default 10 min) ⇒ daemon.exit{reason:
      'idle'}, socket closed, pidfile and socket released only if still ours;
      SIGTERM/SIGINT take the same path with reason 'signal'. Not yet built:
      sessions/subscribers/timers as holders, mod and transport-child stop,
      orphan sweep, the Windows pipe.
    steps:
      - "#gnomon-kernel"
      - "#socket"

aspects:
  ~ample-internal-logs:
    description: >-
      ~ample-internal-logs (spec §9) — everything the kernel does is an event:v1
      row in #kernel-log with the §9.1 default labels set by the appender
      (audience private; turn.event bodies, session.inject bytes,
      approval.request.tool_input, oracle evidence and voice text sensitive;
      a kind without a default is refused, G-LOG-09), and every read goes
      through the §9.3 redaction matrix (stubs {kind, hsid, at, hash} for
      redacting readers, secret never to a read token, nothing to a peer).
      PR-4 covers the label and read-projection halves; retention/TTL
      pruning (§9.4), status metrics (§9.6) and the honesty CI (§9.7) follow.
    category: observability
    severity: high
    tags: [gnomon, logging, redaction, paradigm-8]
    anchors:
      - src/kernel/log/chain.ts
      - src/kernel/log/redaction.ts
    applies-to:
      - "#kernel-log"
